7.0,396109.0,399456.0,412001.0,424698.0,399628.0,417083.0,413000.0,431147.0],"flow":null,"val":null}],"checks":{"sides_minus_open_interest_worst_contracts":0.0,"months":110,"months_without_report_before_start":106},"source":"CFTC Commitments of Traders, Traders in Financial Futures, futures only (dataset gpe5-46if): E-MINI RUSSELL 2000 INDEX - CHICAGO MERCANTILE EXCHANGE, contract code 239742, (russel 2000 index x $50); each month shows its last weekly report (positions as of Tuesday), the weekly reports are in the full table","note":"Each row is how many Russell E-mini futures contracts one kind of trader held short at the last weekly report of each month, and the latest column is the latest report. Open interest is the number of contracts outstanding. Every contract has a buyer and a seller, so the long side and the short side each add up to it, with spread positions (long one delivery month and short another) counted on both sides. These are bets on where US shares go, not holdings: an asset manager long these futures does not own what they are written on. Dealers and banks are mostly on the other side of their clients; asset managers include pension funds, insurers and mutual funds; leveraged funds are mainly hedge funds and trading advisers; small traders hold less than the CFTC's reporting size. Contracts of different futures are different sizes, so counts are compared within one book, never across books. The CFTC has no report for this contract in some earlier months, so the book starts in August 2017; the earlier weeks are in the full table. Source: CFTC Commitments of Traders."}